Here's the set as practice cards. Commit to an answer in each box before you open a hint. A wrong guess you commit to teaches you more than a right answer you only read. Hints start with a nudge, then a worked parallel case on a different molecule, so the last step stays yours.

```show_widget
<div id="app" style="font-family:system-ui,sans-serif;max-width:640px;margin:0 auto;color:inherit">
<style>
.c{border:1px solid rgba(128,128,128,.4);border-radius:10px;padding:12px;margin:0 0 12px}
.q{font-weight:600;margin:0 0 8px;font-size:15px}
textarea{width:100%;box-sizing:border-box;min-height:54px;padding:8px;border-radius:6px;border:1px solid rgba(128,128,128,.5);background:transparent;color:inherit;font:inherit;font-size:14px;resize:vertical}
button{font:inherit;font-size:13px;padding:6px 12px;border-radius:6px;border:1px solid rgba(128,128,128,.6);background:transparent;color:inherit;cursor:pointer;margin:8px 8px 0 0}
button:disabled{opacity:.4;cursor:default}
.h{margin:8px 0 0;padding:8px 10px;border-left:3px solid rgba(100,150,255,.8);font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.45;background:rgba(128,128,128,.1);border-radius:0 6px 6px 0}
#send{font-weight:600;padding:9px 16px;margin-top:4px}
</style>
<div id="cards"></div>
<button id="send">Send my answers for a verdict</button>
<p id="note" style="font-size:12.5px;opacity:.7;margin:8px 0 0">Blank answers are fine; I'll treat them as "no attempt yet."</p>
<script>
var Q=[
{q:"1. Rank by SN2 rate: 1-bromobutane, 2-bromobutane, 2-bromo-2-methylpropane.",
 h:["What does the nucleophile have to reach in an SN2 attack, and from which side? What could physically be in the way?",
    "Parallel case (different molecule): for chloromethane, chloroethane and 2-chloropropane, SN2 rate falls as you add carbon groups on the carbon bearing Cl. Now place your three bromides by the same logic, fastest first."]},
{q:"2. Which is the better leaving group, Cl- or I-? Why?",
 h:["A leaving group departs with the electron pair. What property of the departed ion makes that outcome more comfortable?",
    "Parallel case: HBr is a stronger acid than HF, and Br- is the better leaving group of that pair. What does the acid strength of the conjugate acid tell you about the anion's stability, and which halide is larger and more spread out here?"]},
{q:"3. Predict the product of sodium ethoxide with iodomethane.",
 h:["Name the nucleophile and the electrophilic carbon. Is the substrate hindered, and which mechanism does that favor?",
    "Parallel case: sodium methoxide plus iodoethane gives CH3-O-CH2CH3 and NaI. Apply the same bond-forming and bond-breaking pattern to your reagents, then name the product class."]},
{q:"4. Why does DMSO speed up SN2 compared with methanol?",
 h:["Compare what each solvent does to the anionic nucleophile. Which one can hydrogen-bond to it, and what does that do to its reactivity?",
    "Parallel case: F- is a poor nucleophile in water but quite reactive in acetone. Water cages it, acetone does not. Which of DMSO or methanol behaves like acetone, and what does DMSO do to the cation instead?"]},
{q:"5. What happens to the stereocenter in (R)-2-bromobutane with NaCN?",
 h:["From which side must CN- approach relative to the leaving group? Picture the other three groups on the carbon during that attack.",
    "Parallel case: an umbrella in a gust flips inside out as the wind pushes from the handle side. Apply that to the three groups on the carbon, then decide whether the R/S label changes. Check the CIP priorities of the new substituent instead of assuming."]}
];
var cards=document.getElementById('cards'),tas=[];
Q.forEach(function(it,i){
 var d=document.createElement('div');d.className='c';
 d.innerHTML='<p class="q"></p><textarea placeholder="Your answer and your reasoning"></textarea><div class="hb"></div><div class="hs"></div>';
 d.querySelector('.q').textContent=it.q;
 var ta=d.querySelector('textarea');tas.push(ta);
 var b=document.createElement('button');b.textContent='Hint 1';
 var n=0,hs=d.querySelector('.hs');
 b.onclick=function(){
  if(!ta.value.trim()){b.textContent='Write a guess first';setTimeout(function(){b.textContent=n?'Hint '+(n+1):'Hint 1'},1400);return;}
  var p=document.createElement('div');p.className='h';p.textContent=it.h[n];hs.appendChild(p);n++;
  if(n>=it.h.length){b.disabled=true;b.textContent='No more hints'}else{b.textContent='Hint '+(n+1)}
 };
 d.querySelector('.hb').appendChild(b);cards.appendChild(d);
});
document.getElementById('send').onclick=function(){
 var t="Here are my answers to the practice set:\n"+tas.map(function(x,i){return (i+1)+". "+(x.value.trim()||"(no attempt)")}).join("\n");
 if(typeof sendPrompt==='function')sendPrompt(t);
};
</script>
</div>
```

Take a first pass at all five, then send them. I'll give a verdict on each from my own worked key, not from how confident you sound, and we'll spend our time on the ones that come back "not yet."

If one stumps you completely, write what you do know about it (even just "backside attack, something about sterics"). That tells me where to start.

